{"candidates": [{"conjecture": "The optimal 13-point configuration is uniquely achieved by a genetic algorithm where geometric crossover interpolates corresponding points between two parent configurations via optimal transport matching, and Hall-geometry mutation perturbs a point to the centroid of its largest empty Hall triangle; this converges to a configuration with minimum triangle area 0.00287.", "probability": 0.087}, {"conjecture": "A genetic algorithm using convex-hull-aware crossover (recombining the boundary and interior point-sets of two parents separately) combined with Hall-cell mutation (moving a point to the largest-area cell in its order-3 Hall tessellation) will discover a 13-point configuration whose minimum triangle area equals 1/348 ≈ 0.00287, matching the known lower bound for Heilbronn's problem at n=13.", "probability": 0.079}, {"conjecture": "The globally optimal 13-point arrangement is found by a genetic algorithm employing Voronoi-based geometric crossover that swaps half the Voronoi cells between parents, with mutation that relocates a selected point to the largest-area gap in the order-2 Hall arrangement; the resulting minimum triangle area is 0.00295 and the configuration has D_2 symmetry.", "probability": 0.082}, {"conjecture": "Using a genetic algorithm with Delaunay-triangulation crossover (exchanging Delaunay-edge-connected point subsets between parents) and Hall-geometry mutation targeting the largest empty triangle in the current arrangement's convex hull, the algorithm converges to a configuration with minimum triangle area exactly 1/360, with points distributed on a perturbed triangular lattice.", "probability": 0.074}, {"conjecture": "The optimal 13-point configuration has no nontrivial symmetry and is discovered by genetic algorithm with barycentric crossover (taking weighted averages of parent point-sets after Procrustes alignment) and Hall-triangle mutation; the achieved minimum triangle area is 0.00280, and the configuration is a asymmetric deformation of a 4×4 grid with one point removed.", "probability": 0.068}, {"conjecture": "A genetic algorithm with affine-invariant geometric crossover (recombining parents via random affine transformations of their point-sets) and Hall-geometry mutation (displacing points to maximize the minimum order-3 Hall cell area) will converge to a configuration with minimum triangle area 0.00290, and this configuration places 4 points on the boundary and 9 in the interior.", "probability": 0.071}, {"conjecture": "The optimal solution is found by a genetic algorithm where geometric crossover performs piecewise-linear interpolation between parent configurations matched by minimum-weight bipartite matching, and Hall-geometry mutation replaces the vertex of the minimum-area triangle with the incenter of the largest Hall gap; the resulting minimum triangle area is 0.00278 with 13 points on a slightly perturbed hexagonal lattice.", "probability": 0.065}, {"conjecture": "Genetic algorithm with triangulation-preserving crossover and Hall-geometry mutation converges to a configuration where all 13 points lie on a union of two concentric hexagonal rings plus a center point; the minimum triangle area achieved is 1/375 ≈ 0.00267, and this is within 5% of the global optimum.", "probability": 0.059}, {"conjecture": "The genetic algorithm with geometric crossover defined by spherical interpolation on the doubled-unit-sphere representation of the square, combined with Hall-geometry mutation that perturbs toward the centroid of the maximum-area empty triangle, finds a configuration with minimum triangle area 0.00300; this exceeds the best known construction and the configuration has 4-fold rotational symmetry.", "probability": 0.062}, {"conjecture": "A genetic algorithm using area-weighted Voronoi crossover (inheriting Voronoi cells from parents proportionally to their local minimum-triangle-area contributions) and Hall-geometry mutation (moving points to the largest gap in the order-3 arrangement) will find a 13-point configuration achieving minimum triangle area 0.00285, with 5 points on the boundary forming a regular pentagon and 8 interior points arranged with 4-fold symmetry.", "probability": 0.076}]}
